1
00:00:20,160 --> 00:00:21,470
This is what proof looks like.

2
00:00:21,920 --> 00:00:25,571
QorTime: anti-cheat and verified skill for console games — a signed

3
00:00:25,571 --> 00:00:29,003
receipt tying the score, the footage, and the controller to one

4
00:00:29,003 --> 00:00:29,330
match.

5
00:00:29,620 --> 00:00:32,682
QorCap records the match from the capture card and logs the

6
00:00:32,682 --> 00:00:33,720
controller over USB.

7
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:36,610
The referee signs a receipt, and anyone can check it in

8
00:00:36,610 --> 00:00:37,180
the browser.

9
00:00:37,400 --> 00:00:40,363
Open match one seven zero one two three, and the verifier

10
00:00:40,363 --> 00:00:42,390
pins the trusted referee key by itself.

11
00:00:42,860 --> 00:00:45,310
Verified. Away ten, home nothing.

12
00:00:45,580 --> 00:00:48,390
Meta's Muse Spark explains that result in plain English.

13
00:00:48,580 --> 00:00:51,740
It's signed with a separate advisory key, never the referee key.

14
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:54,030
Edit the explanation, and it disappears.

15
00:00:54,220 --> 00:00:55,380
The verdict doesn't move.

16
00:00:55,620 --> 00:00:57,740
Muse Spark is also the second score reader.

17
00:00:57,940 --> 00:01:01,310
After every locked score, it re-reads the scorebug on its own.

18
00:01:01,520 --> 00:01:04,279
On frames we never trained on, it got seventy three of

19
00:01:04,279 --> 00:01:06,680
seventy three, and was never confidently wrong.

20
00:01:06,900 --> 00:01:08,280
On this match, it agrees.

21
00:01:08,540 --> 00:01:11,280
Now a test: a tampered claim of seven to nothing.

22
00:01:11,480 --> 00:01:14,600
Muse reads three to nothing, and VERIFIED drops to PARTIAL.

23
00:01:14,800 --> 00:01:17,450
It can lower a result, never raise one or fail it.

24
00:01:17,640 --> 00:01:20,640
Tamper with the receipt itself, and the signature breaks. Fail.

25
00:01:21,080 --> 00:01:22,250
About a cent per match.

26
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:23,690
This is Show Your Hands.

27
00:01:23,840 --> 00:01:26,380
The overlay is the real controller log from this match.

28
00:01:26,580 --> 00:01:29,895
Thirty one thousand reports, in four hundred seventy one chunks, each

29
00:01:29,895 --> 00:01:31,240
one hashed into the receipt.

30
00:01:31,440 --> 00:01:34,670
Press-to-pixel asks whether every play start has a press behind it.

31
00:01:34,860 --> 00:01:37,860
Not enough evidence to score — so it scores nothing, instead

32
00:01:37,860 --> 00:01:39,260
of flagging two legit plays.

33
00:01:39,550 --> 00:01:42,570
Meta's SAM three point one draws the scorebug for viewers.

34
00:01:42,750 --> 00:01:45,292
It missed on two of six frames, so it never touches

35
00:01:45,292 --> 00:01:45,790
a verdict.

36
00:01:46,030 --> 00:01:50,320
Muse Spark: explanations, score cross-checks, and play-start filtering.

37
00:01:50,510 --> 00:01:52,230
SAM: the scorebug visual.

38
00:01:52,430 --> 00:01:54,880
Muse Voice Transcribe: the captions you're reading.

39
00:01:55,110 --> 00:01:57,130
We start with Madden, where the data is.

40
00:01:57,330 --> 00:02:00,061
Next on the roadmap, not built yet: shooters like Call of

41
00:02:00,061 --> 00:02:03,079
Duty, where the same receipts could show the player's own stick

42
00:02:03,079 --> 00:02:03,750
drove the aim.

43
00:02:04,050 --> 00:02:07,060
QorTime doesn't prove who held the controller, and it says so.

44
00:02:07,310 --> 00:02:10,199
It does prove the score, the footage, and the inputs belong

45
00:02:10,199 --> 00:02:11,080
to the same match.

46
00:02:11,250 --> 00:02:13,270
That's verified skill. QorTime.
